# Translating a linear system to its augmented matrix

The learner must organize each equation in natural variable order, move variable terms left and constants right, supply implicit unit and missing zero coefficients, then append the constant column. The lesson tutorial 36b1a271-77b9-59ed-9f12-1b6675c26830 defines augmentation; f6081e68-9014-5a0d-a6bf-084abe5fd28e requires natural variable order. The worked canonical example b5967dd6-bdf9-5cd0-9639-ec2816d04256 explicitly organizes, extracts coefficients, and appends constants. The adjacent coefficient-matrix example b2196eb1-6691-549b-950a-a9bab6863a70 supplies prerequisite context; no prerequisite records were supplied separately.

Five easy source profiles have two equations and x,y; five moderate profiles have two or three equations and x,y,z. All use a five-choice radio field. Hard is unsupported. No solving, elimination, row normalization, fractions, diagrams or variable-name extensions are introduced. Equation count, variable count and source term-placement pattern remain fixed per profile to preserve workload. Natural column order and displayed equation order are mathematical conventions, not eligible answer shuffles. Numerical signs, implicit unit locations, missing-variable identities and which coefficient/constant appears at each placement are eligible assignments: the method does not depend on the signs or identities in an example.

Observed evidence: q-71722 (dfed30ca-4b53-5a19-b938-cfe31a91deda) and q-1052 (51bc932c-31ca-5427-ac34-1e2bfeba299b) transpose constants and variable terms. q-129122 (64fc376d-990c-50f9-82bf-f8e85e2ed45f) supplies missing-variable zeros; q-129124 (553e355c-3ed5-592a-b9a8-9897676499b2) mixes placement. q-20361 (608e444e-fb7d-584c-a616-ea55f4a46535) is already organized. Moderate profiles q-1051 (fc7baee7-708d-5083-9b3d-6efab5143302), q-1065 (22e9b9ea-bb31-5721-a113-98ad40800ad3), q-1068 (3760f817-76ad-5963-aefb-d389b1f60dfd), q-1053 (415575f6-a7fc-537e-9b84-fc64413cee34) and q-129128 (ff919820-9ae8-5522-9a28-975841dc4965) use the same stages with three variables. All ten complete source banks and solutions must replay at original literal parameters. Previously omitted profiles are restored from supplied evidence. No mathematical source errors were found; malformed capture delimiters and coefficient-matrix dividers are normalized only in presentation.

Conservative extensions: sample equation/variable-role permutations first, then independently signed integer entries of magnitudes 1..24, preserving the number of missing terms and the multiset of unit magnitudes (units may become constants). Keep each source's term locations on its equation sides, reassigning their variable identities consistently. This changes which terms require sign reversal without adding arithmetic stages. Derive organized equations, both matrices and key from that same instance. The original repeated magnitudes are not a justified coupling constraint.

For sampled banks, use coefficient/constant placement errors, observed particularly in q-71722, q-1052 and q-129128: permute the signed entries of the correct matrix. These authored distractors retain the exact multiset, avoiding unique-number, sign-count, magnitude or zero-count keys. Include cases in which permutations preserve the complete sign/zero mask, so neither the original source mask nor an isolated negative corner can identify the answer. Other cases let signs and missing-variable locations distinguish choices. These banks extend the observed error constructions; they are not represented as the literal source banks. Randomize correct position independently. Source replay retains its complete original bank instead.

Coverage obligations: each profile must exercise both signs in each nonzero entry, both corner signs when the source has a nonzero corner, changed variable/missing-term identities and equation order, unit coefficients, signed boundary magnitudes, and source replay. Controlled relevant-evidence changes must change the prompt, solution, answer and actual grading together; harmless term-order changes must preserve the matrix. Independent parsing must verify every bank and solution. Shortcut checks run within each profile, before and after actual delivery filters. Inventory weaknesses are reproduced separately from new generation; no original assessed source is relabeled generated. Learner selection cannot repair content cues and captured inventory does not supply actual learner history or weights.

## Unit-literal repair (review finding 9ffb0e3daa5aa34af617)

Before this change, the exact q-71722 unit assignment `{'2':1,'3':4}` and its negative-unit counterpart both raise instead of constructing five choices (evidence/unit-before-repair.json); ±2 succeeds. This is a distractor-capacity defect: repeated equal values shrink the sign-preserving permutation orbit below five. The declared signed domain 1..24 remains valid, including collisions and implicit units. The learner still reads/transposes terms, distinguishes implicit 1 from another coefficient, and appends constants; unit inputs must not be discarded.

The lesson introduction 36b1a271-77b9-59ed-9f12-1b6675c26830 says the coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables and the augmentation represents right-hand sides. Source q-20361 (608e444e-fb7d-584c-a616-ea55f4a46535) explicitly uses ±1 coefficients; q-1051 (fc7baee7-708d-5083-9b3d-6efab5143302) and q-129122 (64fc376d-990c-50f9-82bf-f8e85e2ed45f) distinguish missing zero coefficients from ones. These support coefficient-reading distractors as well as the previously used placement errors.

Retain existing distinct same-sign permutations. If fewer than five exist, supplement them with single-entry magnitude-reading errors, preserving signs and missing-zero locations. Change a nonzero coefficient or constant by one in absolute magnitude, keeping it nonzero; its location and error direction are eligible assignments. This is an authored extension, not a claim about an original bank recipe. It adds no learner calculation or solution stage. All choices still share the full sign/zero mask, so neither the old corner cue nor a memorized sign pattern isolates the key. Where permutations exist, retain those alternatives so an unchanged multiset does not uniquely identify the answer. If all entries of each sign are equal, direct recognition of repeated/implicit coefficients is a legitimate simplification of this skill; do not exclude it or claim it tests difficult placement distinctions.

Affected checks must exercise positive and negative unit literals, repeated magnitudes including fully collapsed literals, adjacent nonunit controls, magnitude endpoints, both supported difficulty bands, and changes of the decisive coefficient with all other input held fixed. Independently parse visible prompts and solution matrices, check all five banks and native radio grades, and compare shortcut probes before/after delivery. Exact source replay and the ordinary sampled construction are retained.

